= 1
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Use <Gaussian units>, uniform <number density> $n$, and <ion> <mass density> $\rho=nm_i$. Here $e>0$ is the magnitude of the elementary charge, as fixed by the printed current relation. Neglect <electron> inertia and displacement current. Then
$$
\mathbf j=\frac c{4\pi}\nabla\times\mathbf B,\qquad
\mathbf u_e=\mathbf u-\frac c{4\pi en}\nabla\times\mathbf B.
$$
Freezing the <magnetic flux> into this <electron> velocity, while retaining the <ion> momentum equation, gives the closed <Hall magnetohydrodynamics> system
$$
\boxed{\partial_t\mathbf B=\nabla\times\left[\left(\mathbf u-\frac c{4\pi en}\nabla\times\mathbf B\right)\times\mathbf B\right],}
$$
$$
\boxed{\partial_t\mathbf u+(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u=-\frac{\nabla p}{\rho}+\frac{(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B}{4\pi\rho},\qquad\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0.}
$$
The <pressure> enforces the <incompressible flow> constraint; taking the divergence of momentum determines it up to the usual boundary-dependent constant. The induction equation preserves the <solenoidal magnetic-field constraint>. One can absorb $B^2/(8\pi)$ into <magnetohydrodynamic total pressure>, replacing the Lorentz term by <magnetic tension>. Keeping <ion> inertia here is important: <electron magnetohydrodynamics> with fixed <ions> will be a later limit, not the complete two-field system.

= 2
{parent=Paper 75/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Linearize about zero bulk flow and the uniform guide field. Put $\mathbf b=\delta\mathbf B/\sqrt{4\pi\rho}$, and take <plane waves> proportional to $e^{i(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x-\omega t)}$. Let $k_\parallel=\mathbf k\cdot\hat{\mathbf z}$, $k=|\mathbf k|$, $q=k_\parallel v_A$, and
$$
d_i=\frac c{\omega_{pi}},\qquad v_Ad_i=\frac{cB_0}{4\pi en}.
$$
Both $\mathbf u$ and $\mathbf b$ are perpendicular to $\mathbf k$. Projecting momentum onto that plane eliminates total <pressure>. The remaining magnetic-tension term gives
$$
-i\omega\mathbf u=iq\mathbf b,\qquad \omega\mathbf u=-q\mathbf b.
$$
In the induction equation, the Hall contribution is $-c(B_0\partial_z)(\nabla\times\delta\mathbf B)/(4\pi en)$; its two derivative factors of $i$ in the <Fourier mode> produce a positive coefficient multiplying $\mathbf k\times\mathbf b$. Thus
$$
-i\omega\mathbf b=iq\mathbf u+qd_i\mathbf k\times\mathbf b,
\qquad\omega\mathbf b=-q\mathbf u+i qd_i\mathbf k\times\mathbf b.
$$
Eliminating $\mathbf u$ gives
$$
(\omega^2-q^2)\mathbf b=i\omega qd_i\mathbf k\times\mathbf b.
$$
On the transverse plane, $(i\mathbf k\times)^2=k^2$, so applying that operator again proves the <incompressible Hall-MHD wave dispersion>:
$$
\boxed{(\omega^2-k_\parallel^2v_A^2)^2=\omega^2 k_\parallel^2v_A^2k^2\frac{c^2}{\omega_{pi}^2}.}
$$
Equivalently, in a <helicity decomposition of a transverse Fourier mode> with $i\mathbf k\times\mathbf b_h=hk\mathbf b_h$, $h=\pm1$, each polarization obeys $\omega^2-hqkd_i\omega-q^2=0$. For $k_\parallel=0$ the projected linear restoring terms vanish and these modes have zero frequency; the propagating calculation assumes $k_\parallel\ne0$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 75/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Solving the polarization quadratic gives all four signed roots:
$$
\boxed{\omega_{h,s}=\frac q2\left(hkd_i+s\sqrt{4+k^2d_i^2}\right),\qquad h,s=\pm1.}
$$
A sign-independent description is the pair of positive frequency magnitudes
$$
\boxed{\Omega_{\rm fast,slow}=|k_\parallel|v_A\left[\sqrt{1+\frac{k^2d_i^2}{4}}\ \pm\ \frac{kd_i}{2}\right].}
$$
For wavelengths long compared with the <ion skin depth>, $kd_i\ll1$, both magnitudes approach $|k_\parallel|v_A$. The Hall correction is then small and the usual <Alfvén wave> dispersion $\omega^2=k_\parallel^2v_A^2$ is recovered. In an incompressible system the two transverse polarizations include the shear and <pseudo-Alfvén wave> sectors; away from this limit Hall coupling selects circular helicity combinations. A small parallel wavenumber alone does not remove the relative Hall splitting: the expansion parameter is the full $kd_i$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 75/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

When $kd_i\gg1$, expand the square root in the exact frequencies. The fast branch has
$$
\Omega_{\rm fast}=|q|\left(kd_i+\frac1{kd_i}+O((kd_i)^{-3})\right),
$$
while the slow branch has $\Omega_{\rm slow}=|q|[(kd_i)^{-1}+O((kd_i)^{-3})]$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\omega_{\rm fast}^2\simeq k_\parallel^2v_A^2k^2d_i^2,\qquad
\omega_{\rm slow}^2\simeq\frac{k_\parallel^2v_A^2}{k^2d_i^2}.}
$$
The fast dispersive wave is the mode sought in the question's subsequent fast-time limit. In a strongly oblique wave, $k\simeq k_\perp$, so its frequency scales as $|\omega|\simeq|k_\parallel|v_Ak_\perp d_i$. The slow branch instead tends to $(|k_\parallel|/k)\Omega_i$, where the <ion> <cyclotron frequency> is $\Omega_i=v_A/d_i=eB_0/(m_ic)$.

There is a terminology qualification: in the pressure-free incompressible Hall equations used here, the fast branch is conventionally a <whistler wave>. The question calls the requested small-scale waves <kinetic Alfvén waves>, but a general kinetic-Alfvén dispersion includes <pressure>/kinetic ordering absent from these equations. The boxed dispersion is the result for the printed model; no extra <pressure> response or kinetic term has been silently added.

= 5
{parent=Paper 75/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Write $\epsilon=l/d_i\ll1$ and $\delta=v_A\tau/l\ll1$. For magnetic amplitudes of order $B_0$, <ion> acceleration is of order $v_A^2/l$. Thus its change over the fast time is
$$
\Delta u\sim\frac{v_A^2\tau}{l}=\delta v_A\ll v_A.
$$
The <ions> are consequently fixed to leading order on this timescale. Their first nonzero response is driven by the momentum equation, with negligible self-advection for an initially resting <ion> flow; one need not assert that the dimensional Lorentz acceleration literally vanishes. In fast-time, Alfvén-velocity dimensionless variables, the <ion> equation reads $\partial_{\hat t}\hat{\mathbf u}=O(\delta)$, giving $\partial_{\hat t}\hat{\mathbf u}=0$ at leading order.

Meanwhile the electron-current velocity is $j/(en)\sim v_Ad_i/l=v_A/\epsilon$, larger than a bulk flow of order $v_A$. The ion-advection term in induction is smaller than Hall induction by $O(\epsilon)$ even if that slow flow is retained. The leading magnetic equation is therefore
$$
\boxed{\partial_t\mathbf B=-\frac c{4\pi en}\nabla\times[(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B],\qquad\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0,}
$$
with stationary <ions> at leading order. This is the <fast-time electron-MHD limit of Hall magnetohydrodynamics> and is precisely <electron magnetohydrodynamics>.

The already obtained fast-wave eigenvector has $u/b=|q/\omega|\sim1/(kd_i)\ll1$, confirming that <ion> motion cannot affect its leading frequency. The closed magnetic equation therefore suffices to obtain that branch without using momentum to close it. Its timescale is $\tau_w\sim1/(v_Ad_i|k_\parallel|k)$; compatibility with $\tau_w\ll l/v_A$ requires $|k_\parallel|d_i\gg1$ when $l\sim k^{-1}$. Arbitrarily small $k_\parallel$ can violate this fast-time assumption even though $kd_i\gg1$. The slow branch does not obey the stated ordering and is not retained by the fixed-ion reduction.

